# debugging (Cursor)

A disciplined loop for finding *why* something is broken, not just making
the symptom disappear.

## When NOT to use

Writing a brand-new feature (no bug to investigate — use
`incremental-implementation`/`test-driven-development`); reviewing code
with no known bug (use `code-review-and-quality`); the cause is already
known and it's just applying the fix (skip the full loop, just fix it).

## Steps

1. **Reproduce it first, before touching any code.** Don't change anything
   until the bug triggers consistently. If it can't be reproduced after
   reasonable effort, report that as a finding — don't guess a fix for an
   unconfirmed bug. For intermittent bugs, look for a pattern (specific
   conditions, race, timing, environment) before treating it as random.
2. **Read the error/stack trace all the way through before assuming
   anything.** The first line is often not the root cause — follow it to
   the frame in the project's own code, read the message literally.
3. **Isolate to the smallest case that still fails.** Strip irrelevant
   variables one at a time (smaller data, disable other features, call the
   function directly instead of the full UI flow).
4. **Bisect to the change that caused it, for regressions.** Use `git log`
   on the relevant file or `git bisect` when the range isn't obvious —
   don't guess which commit broke it.
5. **Form a hypothesis, then verify it before writing a fix.** Temporary
   logging, a breakpoint, or an assertion to confirm the suspected cause —
   a fix pasted without verifying the cause often just papers over the
   symptom.
6. **Tell symptom apart from root cause.** If the fix is a
   null-check/try-catch right at the crash site, ask why the value is
   null/invalid there in the first place — fix at the source if there's a
   deeper preventable cause.
7. **Verify the fix closes the case from steps 1 and 3**, then run the
   relevant test suite (not just the fixed case) to check for new
   regressions. Write a regression test if none exists yet.
8. **Report the finding, not just "it's fixed now"** — root cause, why it
   happened, what changed, especially if the fix touches a wider area than
   the original crash site.

## When reproduction fails after reasonable attempts

Don't keep guessing fixes at random. Report what's been tried, what
signals exist, and what additional information is needed — a stuck
investigation is a valid finding, not something to paper over with a
speculative fix.
